const newClusters = [
  { subject: 'Business Studies', domain: 'Management', ageRangeStart: 17, ageRangeEnd: 18,
    summary: 'The Class XII course treats management as a process with five linked functions rather than a job title. Planning decides what to do, organising builds the structure, staffing fills it, directing gets people to act and controlling checks whether any of it worked. The connection that matters most is between the first and the last: control is meaningless without a plan to measure against, which is why the two are usually taught as a pair.' },
  { subject: 'Economics', domain: 'Macroeconomics', ageRangeStart: 17, ageRangeEnd: 18,
    summary: 'Macroeconomics steps back from single markets to the economy as a whole. Learners first meet the national accounts and the three equivalent ways of measuring output, then the idea that total demand determines how much gets produced and how many people work. From there the pieces connect: banks create the money that finances spending, government budgets add or withdraw demand, and the balance of payments records what crosses the border. The multiplier is the idea that ties it together — spending becomes someone’s income, which becomes further spending.' },
  { subject: 'Accountancy', domain: 'Partnership Accounts', ageRangeStart: 17, ageRangeEnd: 18,
    summary: 'Partnership accounting is where the mechanics learned in Class XI meet the awkward realities of shared ownership. Profits have to be divided according to an agreement — or according to default legal provisions when partners never wrote one down. When someone joins, leaves or dies, the firm has to value what it has built up that does not appear on the balance sheet, which is what goodwill represents, and revalue assets that have drifted from their recorded amounts.' },
];

